Add a disk-size cap + LRU eviction to the fs KV-offload secondary tier.

Full investigation: docs/mamba-align-prefill-leak.md's "no disk cap"
section. vLLM 0.27.1's FileSystemTierManager (v1/kv_offload/tiering/fs/
manager.py) and FileMapper (v1/kv_offload/file_mapper.py) have no size
limit or eviction policy at all -- unlike the primary CPU tier
(cpu_bytes_to_use, LRU/ARC eviction), the fs secondary tier just writes
block files to root_dir forever. On a long-running server this fills the
disk in hours to days at typical KV-cache-offload data rates, not
something an external periodic script (prune_kv_offload_cache.sh, this
repo's earlier attempt) can keep up with -- the growth is continuous
while the server runs, so the enforcement has to be too.

New FileSystemTierManager.__init__ kwargs (both optional, default
0/backward-compatible -- zero behavior change unless configured):

  max_disk_gib: cap this tier's on-disk usage (this rank's own block
    directory, "<base_path>_r<rank>/") at approximately this many GiB.
  disk_cap_check_interval_s: how often to re-check (default 120s).

When max_disk_gib > 0, a dedicated daemon thread (vllm_kv_fs_disk_cap)
periodically walks this rank's block directory and deletes the least-
recently-*accessed* (atime, not mtime -- a real recency signal as long
as the filesystem isn't mounted noatime; relatime, the common default,
is sufficient) *.bin files until usage is back at or under the cap.
Deliberately runs off the main scheduling thread and only on a slow
timer: the scan is a full recursive directory walk, and this code lives
directly downstream of a subsystem (align-mode Mamba + KV offloading)
that this investigation found to be extremely sensitive to anything
synchronous stalling the scheduler -- see the mamba-align doc's hang
investigation. A background thread bounded by a long interval can never
have that failure mode, unlike a synchronous check hooked into
get_finished_jobs() or lookup().

Safe to race concurrent stores/reads/promotions on a live server:
deleting a file that's mid-read just produces one ordinary MISS for that
lookup (the connector already handles MISS gracefully, confirmed
extensively elsewhere in this investigation), never a crash or torn
read, since block files are only ever removed whole by filename, never
truncated or rewritten in place.

Reference design: CachyLLama's --cache-ssd-cold-maxsize
(~/git/cachy-fork, tools/server/server-context-page-manager.cpp) --
global byte cap, oldest-mtime-first eviction of whole conversation
directories, enforced under the existing lock after each store. Ported
the core idea (a size cap with LRU-style eviction, opt-in, default
unlimited) but not the implementation: CachyLLama needs an in-memory
index of on-disk state (and had to fix a real bug where that index went
stale across process restarts, see its commit 162663327 -- lesson
applied here from the start) because its checkpoints carry additional
per-slot metadata; vLLM's fs tier has no equivalent index at all --
os.path.exists() on a deterministic content-hash path *is* the whole
lookup mechanism -- so a plain filesystem walk is already the correct
source of truth here, with no bookkeeping to keep in sync.

Verified (2026-08-22) with a standalone test against the real eviction
method (bypassing full engine/model construction): correctly evicts
exactly the oldest-atime files down to the target cap, correctly no-ops
when already under cap re-run immediately after, correctly leaves the
per-run config.json (one directory level up, not a *.bin file) alone,
correctly handles a not-yet-existing directory without raising, and the
background-thread start/stop lifecycle joins cleanly with no hang.
NOT yet verified against a live model-loaded server (this session was
asked to code but not launch vLLM while the user tests the two prior
fixes concurrently) -- please verify a real run's log line looks sane
before relying on it unattended:
  KV offload fs tier '<type>': capping on-disk usage at N GiB ...
and, once the cap is actually exceeded in practice:
  KV offload fs tier: disk cap exceeded (...) -- evicted N ...

Wired up in micke-start.sh's kv-transfer-config JSON: added
"max_disk_gib":200 to the fs secondary_tiers entry.

